<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kabuki">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kabuki</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kabuki is a form of Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> combining drama, music and dance, known for stylised acting and elaborate costumes. It developed in the early seventeenth century and flourished in the urban culture of Edo. It is among the cultural legacies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kabuki.jpg" alt="Kabuki" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morita-za_in_the_1790s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
